  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.4 z²) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. Left to right (x) is steep, front to back (z) is gentle. Without noise, x overshoots for learning rates above 0.42 and grows for rates above 0.83.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 0.8 × √((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at b = 64. It comes from the fixed seed, and each new run uses the next values.",
    "Both arrows show steps (learning rate × slope estimate), not slopes, drawn 4 times their true length with the same scale for both. Mini-batch step is where the ball moves next. Full-data step uses the true slope.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is marked diverged when the loss goes above 4.8 (the loss at the steep rim) or the ball leaves the bowl front to back. Off-bowl positions are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average. The trail holds the 40 landing points of this run, and the ghost holds the last run kept with New run."
  ]
}